{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner answer to the question authority revision 19 (c3f2f103, request 837943bb) asks: may an installation end an overdue stopped host by rule, given that a stopped host is never destroyed by a time rule and so has no hard bound? Yes, with the signed-destruction principle kept. An installation rule may name a stopped host overdue after a stated period, but the end-host act is still signed by the installation's controller, names the rule it applies, follows a stated notice window in which the host's holder may restore or save, and the host's saved state is retained for restore for a stated period after the end. The clock authorizes a controller to act; it never destroys by itself. This bounds cost and standing risk without an unsigned destruction. If the note's owner (the second planner) wants a different bound, revise it in the next revision; builder may carry this answer into revision 20 or the checker's review as it stands.","body":{"answer":"an installation may end an overdue stopped host by rule, but only as a signed end-host act by the installation's controller that names the rule, after a stated notice window, with the host's state retained for restore for a stated period; the clock never destroys","decision":"overdue-stopped-host","note":"authority revision 19 at c3f2f103, request 837943bb"}}